# Audtheia V2 line-ending and binary policy.
#
# Shell scripts are pinned to LF because they are the one real cross-platform
# risk in this project. The desktop archives the working tree and copies it to a
# Raspberry Pi, where a script checked out with Windows line endings will not run
# under the Pi's shell. Pinning these to LF keeps a station's setup and launch
# scripts valid no matter what a contributor's core.autocrlf setting is. They are
# already stored with LF, so declaring this changes no committed content.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.command   text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are launched only on Windows, where the command processor
# reads them whatever their line endings, and they are never sent to a field
# station, so they carry no cross-platform risk. They are left to a contributor's
# own checkout settings rather than being renormalized here, which keeps this
# change additive.

# Configuration and data files are pinned to LF because the application writes
# them itself. A settings file saved on a Windows desktop and the same file saved
# on a field station must not differ byte for byte over invisible characters, and
# a rewritten line ending must never appear in version control as a wholly
# changed file that hides the one value that actually changed.
*.json      text eol=lf

# Never translate line endings inside binary artifacts, so a model, database, or
# image that ever sits beside the code stays byte-for-byte intact. Version
# control ignores these today; the rule is a safeguard for anything staged by
# hand later, such as a tray or web icon.
*.hef       binary
*.onnx      binary
*.gguf      binary
*.tflite    binary
*.db        binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
